#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124330 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124521 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9d77214-796e-558f-a364-00ae3e25cc13" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "d135b6c2-8257-5ba5-ac15-3d43a9abd3d8" :question/math-academy-id "e-5444" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true given that $\\mathbf{T}:\\mathbb{R}^3 \\to \\mathbb{R}^3$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T,$ and $T$ is invertible? \n\nI. $\\textrm{Ker}(\\mathbf{T}) \\neq \\{\\mathbf{0}\\}$\n\nII. $\\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T})=0$\n\nIII. $\\textrm{rank}(\\mathbf{T}) = 3$" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\mathbf{T}$ is a linear transformation from $\\mathbb{R}^3$ into $\\mathbb{R}^3,$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $\\mathbf{T}$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) ![](images/3e/3eba90e8e26e611656e00139533ea0e91628b7d53316fe0fbb33961d75211d04.svg) \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $T$ is invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\textrm{Ker}(\\mathbf{T}) = \\{\\mathbf{0}\\}.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $T$ is invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T}) = 0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $T$ is invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\textrm{rank}(\\mathbf{T}) = 3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124435 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124631 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "113a601e-220a-54a3-a64b-1e554bc3d79f" :knowledge-point/title "Сompleting a Sentence Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "2086adfe-9a09-5b60-8f5e-9edb0c700b98" :question/math-academy-id "e-9985" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{T}:\\mathbb{R}^3\\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}^3$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ Consider the following statement:\n\n*If $[1, 2, 1]^T \\not\\in  \\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T}),$ then $\\textrm{__________}.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\nI. $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})=\\mathbb{R}^3$\n\nII. $\\textrm{Ker}(\\mathbf{T})\\ne\\{\\mathbf{0}\\}$\n\nIII. $\\mathbf{T}$ is invertible" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\mathbf{T}$ is a linear transformation from $\\mathbb{R}^3$ into $\\mathbb{R}^3,$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $\\mathbf{T}$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) ![](images/d5/d54f9467c22c22ade32bbb49f611c545140b9ce5d11610c04dd7f946d83ac789.svg) \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $[1, 2, 1]^T \\not\\in  \\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T}),$ we must have that $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T}) \\neq \\mathbb{R}^3.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since the vector $[1, 2, 1]^T$ does not lie in $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})$, then $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T}) \\neq \\mathbb{R}^3.$ Therefore, according to the invertible matrix theorem, we must have $\\textrm{Ker}(\\mathbf{T}) \\neq \\{\\mathbf{0}\\}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T}) \\neq \\mathbb{R}^3,$ the transformation $\\mathbf T$ can't be invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124436 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124632 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eead7563-6231-5490-9a10-721d09133352" :knowledge-point/title "Recognizing Parts of the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "427d3dc9-9ae8-5c40-b75c-519db84f2bc6" :question/math-academy-id "e-9986" :question/problem "$\\mathbf{T}$ is a bijection ![](images/29/29402a77d81267af571672df82b86e1d9853f17f26b6ad0a139a0d0f4ea906c7.svg) \n$\\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad$  $T$ is invertible\n\nConsider the equivalence above, where $\\mathbf{T}:\\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}^n$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ What should be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the *invertible matrix theorem* (in terms of linear transformations) states the following: \n\n $\\mathbf{T}$ is a bijection ![](images/dd/dd3981fa517de2829a94c8b50c09e66a9f9830a3ddcf2651b583fb4da348c93c.svg) \n$\\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad$  $T$ is invertible\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272021 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124631 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "113a601e-220a-54a3-a64b-1e554bc3d79f" :knowledge-point/title "Сompleting a Sentence Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e440973-55fe-51fa-9da0-f4d52cf2e1f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269685} {:answer/id #uuid "6564e814-315d-5d94-906f-1b6ff52f2ea8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270172} {:answer/id #uuid "af1a5bdd-ecdf-550d-aa85-b91ee12261f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270825} {:answer/id #uuid "cc38f4d0-c9ec-5bea-9900-3e87852c1726" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271062} {:answer/id #uuid "eb8a718e-676e-5690-ab51-91d91cda239d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271338}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af1a5bdd-ecdf-550d-aa85-b91ee12261f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270825} :answer-field/id #uuid "bab21a93-10c6-5a81-9fe7-a6f973ee6db2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271848}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1cac76f2-abeb-58d4-87e9-5f3619bedd4c" :question/math-academy-id "q-140085" :question/problem "Let $T:{R}^{5}\\to {R}^{5}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ Consider the following statement:\n\n*If $\\text{rank}(T)=5,$ then $\\text{__________}.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\nI. $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\}$\n\nII. $T$ is invertible\n\nIII. $\\text{nullity}(T)=0$" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{5}$ into ${R}^{5},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{5} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=5 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $\\text{rank}(T)=5,$ the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\}.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, since $\\text{rank}(T)=5,$ the invertible matrix theorem implies that $T$ is invertible.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $T$ is invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{nullity}(T)=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459267 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124632 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eead7563-6231-5490-9a10-721d09133352" :knowledge-point/title "Recognizing Parts of the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d66ce99-4b24-5fa0-b6df-6ff7a5661d8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 1