$ and $\\text{Ker}(T).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459301 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124632 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eead7563-6231-5490-9a10-721d09133352" :knowledge-point/title "Recognizing Parts of the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ad91a4a-06f2-5d9a-8655-5263c848d3f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\{0\\}$ and $n$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458640} {:answer/id #uuid "0e8919b8-5643-5b79-8930-830170d078aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}^{n}$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458649} {:answer/id #uuid "4c867958-e95e-5917-a788-9cd854240b91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\{0\\}$ and $0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458766} {:answer/id #uuid "972660c3-34f0-56ad-abc9-d0eb75375474" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R∖\\{0\\}$ and $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458893} {:answer/id #uuid "d66b85e5-4ade-5bb2-8d27-85b829c3c7d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ and $0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459002}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c867958-e95e-5917-a788-9cd854240b91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\{0\\}$ and $0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458766} :answer-field/id #uuid "712c7f36-648a-59ce-b5e6-2d0dcc22e8d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459134}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f070189a-9c50-508c-9a92-9de0a01ed5ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-141456" :question/problem "$T$ is a bijection $\\,⟺\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=n \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\bbox[3pt,white,border: 2px solid Gainsboro]{\\phantom{W}} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=\\bbox[3pt,white,border: 2px solid Gainsboro]{\\phantom{W}}\\end{aligned}$ \n$\\,⟺\\,$  $T$ is invertible\n\nConsider the equivalence above, where $T:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ **From top to bottom**, which of the following should be placed into the blank spaces to make the statements true?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the *invertible matrix theorem* (in terms of linear transformations) states the following: \n\n $T$ is a bijection $\\,⟺\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=n \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n$\\,⟺\\,$  $T$ is invertible\n\nTherefore, from top to bottom, the missing expression are $\\{0\\}$ and $0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272285 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124632 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eead7563-6231-5490-9a10-721d09133352" :knowledge-point/title "Recognizing Parts of the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1af878bc-a2d4-5d29-9f1c-9e4a3cf3f993" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{Domain}(T)$ and $n-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269487} {:answer/id #uuid "b89bcde2-5886-5353-b2fb-ee39f615fc0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}^{n}$ and $\\text{det}(T)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270900} {:answer/id #uuid "baa5abde-92f5-5f9a-af2b-a0a103048cab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0$ and $n$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270915} {:answer/id #uuid "e3cd4fc3-738a-5af8-a034-a398ae8afd2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}^{n}$ and $n$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271274}