entifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3200c704-914e-5a7c-a389-b3b6182e8ac4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458724} {:answer/id #uuid "37e3e013-4933-5b45-8470-5d76dafc6885" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458736} {:answer/id #uuid "923f09df-dc7e-5a40-ae18-a729158db7a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458884} {:answer/id #uuid "d8489c3f-9e56-5d17-962f-9c79515a686c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459006} {:answer/id #uuid "f3ac2196-f618-5a19-8037-4a5f973800f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459059}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d8489c3f-9e56-5d17-962f-9c79515a686c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459006} :answer-field/id #uuid "00203bb5-c9e3-5903-82ca-f2bb945cbf8b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459087}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6afa206b-a720-5d99-9269-3c50ce4125f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-141458" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true given that $T:{R}^{5}\\to {R}^{5}$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T,$ and $T({v}_{1})=T({v}_{2})$ for some distinct vectors  ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}?$ \n\nI. $\\text{nullity}(T)=0$\n\nII. $T$ is a bijection\n\nIII. $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{5}$" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{5}$ into ${R}^{5},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{5} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=5 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nSince $T$ is a linear transformation, we have that\n\n$$\nT({v}_{1})=T({v}_{2})\\,⟹\\,T({v}_{1}-{v}_{2})=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since ${v}_{1}-{v}_{2}\\in \\text{Ker}(T)$ and ${v}_{1}\\ne {v}_{2},$ we must have that $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\},$ which means  $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0,$ the transformation $T$ is not a bijection.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $T$ is not a bijection, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{5}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272287 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124521 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9d77214-796e-558f-a364-00ae3e25cc13" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f6e6c5d7-e923-5b79-aeca-92ce53c73074" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449640} {:answer/id #uuid "709274c3-c137-53e6-a5e6-794fdfe9a85a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449641}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "709274c3-c137-53e6-a5e6-794fdfe9a85a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449641} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d40a1ee-aefc-57fa-8071-7deb8e4a3305" :answer-fi