e given that $T:{R}^{7}\\to {R}^{7}$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T,$ and $T(x)=v$ has no solution for some vector $v?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $T$ is not invertible\n- **II.** $\\text{nullity}(T)=0$\n- **III.** $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 7$" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{7}$ into ${R}^{7},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{7} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=7 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $T(x)=v$ has no solution, we have that $v\\notin \\text{Im}(T),$ hence $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{7}.$ According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{7},$ the transformation $T$ and the matrix $T$ are not invertible.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $T$ is not invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $T$ is not invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 7.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459233 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124631 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "113a601e-220a-54a3-a64b-1e554bc3d79f" :knowledge-point/title "Сompleting a Sentence Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "422924b4-52fe-531f-a431-2930ed3fcc1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458750} {:answer/id #uuid "46707c39-a729-5f37-b02d-6b6349de020e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458756} {:answer/id #uuid "80cb7339-73c7-5e3e-8a6c-b8959ca9790c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458854} {:answer/id #uuid "b663eb34-b00f-59e8-bb33-8fbde678b6de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458956} {:answer/id #uuid "e5fb6fad-edf8-5bd5-8196-28ca6189208d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459028}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "422924b4-52fe-531f-a431-2930ed3fcc1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458750} :answer-field/id #uuid "374d59dc-859f-58b1-91a1-44f364cb378e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459112}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4841bd4c-e53a-5f1a-ae44-1f39564910ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-141485" :question/problem "Let $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ Consider the following statement:\n\n*If $T$ is not a bijection, then $\\text{__________}.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\nI. $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{3}$\n\nII. $\\text{rank}(T)=3$\n\nIII. $T(v)=0$ for some $v\\ne 0$" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $T$ is not a bijection, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{3}.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $T$ is not a bijection, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 3.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $T$ is not a bijection, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\}.$ In turn, there is $v\\ne 0$ such that $T(v)=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272291 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124631 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "113a601e-220a-54a3-a64b-1e554bc3d79f" :knowledge-point/title "Сompleting a Sentence Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "429117a3-6e54-516f-90dc-8b3c46852d73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269870} {:answer/id #uuid "540ff262-b6d8-55d6-bdff-7a8f7374caf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270017} {:answer/id #uuid "953e5319-0e76-5d91-be9e-97acbe17adc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270577} {:answer/id #uuid "c637029f-ce11-54ac-92f8-b41a82831cf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271015} {:answer/id #uuid "f27f32f7-f9d9-50bf-b772-90b1d8509143" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271401}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f27f32f7-f9d9-50bf-b772-90b1d8509143" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271401} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f70c062-2c20-514b-a050-6ed7cd19ecf6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271795}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "496f9381-a057-5d31-a929-726bf731d70b" :question/math-academy-id "q-141495" :question/problem "Let $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T,$ and ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\in {R}^{3}.$  Consider the following statement:\n\n*If $2T({v}_{1})-3T({v}_{2})=0$ and ${v}_{1}\\ne k{v}_{2}$ for all $k\\in R$ then $\\text{__________}.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\nI. $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\}$\n\nII. $\\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3}$\n\nIII. $T$ is invertible" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $2T({v}_{1})-3T({v}_{2})=0$ and $T$ is a linear transformation, we have $T(2{v}_{1}-3{v}_{2})=0$ which means $2{v}_{1}-3{v}_{2}\\in \\text{Ker}(T).$   Since ${v}_{1}\\ne k{v}_{2}$,  we must have $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\}.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, the transformation $T$ is not a bijection. Since $T$ is not a bijection, then by the invertible matrix theorem, $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{3}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{3},$ the corresponding matrix $T$ can't be invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125118 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124521 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9d77214-796e-558f-a364-00ae3e25cc13" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0378cd55-aff8-50e6-9d25-9722e86e2c06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364133} {:answer/id #uuid "47f7fcda-7cdd-5fac-b178-9b5279c586b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364134} {:answer/id #uuid "f8f6eded-d14d-5102-a8cf-e9c780b59fc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364135} {:answer/id #uuid "c87266eb-6f2d-5861-bb16-88682d737679" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements is true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364136} {:answer/id #uuid "65456865-d282-5ff1-baf8-1c617cf373ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364137}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f8f6eded-d14d-5102-a8cf-e9c780b59fc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364135} :answer-field/id #uuid "1888a5e7-c78d-5503-b0b1-14a906b14550" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364132}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d2b1c7be-1664-54ba-8015-a807eb2f1187" :question/math-academy-id "q-38033" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true given that $T:{R}^{8}\\to {R}^{8}$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T,$ and $T({v}_{1})=T({v}_{2})$ for some distinct vectors  ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}?$ \n\nI. $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\}$\n\nII. $T$ is invertible\n\nIII. $\\text{rank}(T)<8$" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{8}$ into ${R}^{8},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{8} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=8 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nSince $T$ is a linear transformation, we have that\n\n$$\nT({v}_{1})=T({v}_{2})\\,⟹\\,T({v}_{1}-{v}_{2})=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since ${v}_{1}-{v}_{2}\\in \\text{Ker}(T)$ and ${v}_{1}\\ne {v}_{2},$ we must have that $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\}.$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\},$ the transformation $T$ and the matrix $T$ are not invertible.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $T$ is not invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 8.$ In turn, this means that $\\text{rank}(T)<8.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364145 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124631 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "113a601e-220a-54a3-a64b-1e554bc3d79f" :knowledge-point/title "Сompleting a Sentence Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b5f07bf4-4a80-5c38-8806-6cbd8d26fa57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364147} {:answer/id #uuid "862b97ed-b399-5806-9f72-3a71bd6e9f7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364148} {:answer/id #uuid "557f525c-a15e-58a6-9656-27d5203e43a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 175921863641